markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Managing the Project 

## Daily Spot Checks, Warm-Ups, and Exit Tickets

Use daily structures to quickly assess team progress, collaboration, and understanding without adding extra grading workload.

---

## Warm-Ups as Daily Status Checks

The beginning of class is the perfect time to evaluate how teams are managing workflow and collaboration. 

**Use these prompts to spot-check team alignment before work begins:**

<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 10px; margin: 20px 0px;">

  <div style="background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 12pt;">Day 2: Checking Task Quality</p>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Ask: <i>“Are these ready to be task cards? If not, how would you rewrite them?”</i></p>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Look for tasks that are <b>specific, actionable, and small enough</b> to complete in one class period.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 12pt;"">Day 6: Checking Kanban Accuracy</p>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Ask teams to identify:</p>
    <ul>
      <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">One card that moved forward</li>
      <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">One card that’s stuck</li>
      <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">One card to add or split</li>
    </ul>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Prevents the board from becoming static.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;font-size: 12pt;">Day 7: Checking Progress Reality</p>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Ask: <i>“On track, some concerns, or blocked? What made you choose that?”</i></p>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Surfaces where teams need support or scope adjustments.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;font-size: 12pt;">Day 9: Checking Prioritization</p>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Ask: <i>“If you had 10 extra minutes, what would you work on?”</i></p>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Pushes teams to prioritize <b>high-value unfinished work</b>.</p>
  </div>

</div>

---

## Circulation Questions (Over-the-Shoulder Spot Checks)

Instead of asking “Are you done?”, use targeted questions to assess understanding and decision-making.

### During Planning (Days 1–2)

- “What are you intentionally leaving out of this version?”
- “If I hadn’t seen your project before, could I follow what happens?”

### During Building & Testing (Days 4–7)

- “What did you learn from testing this interaction?”
- “Did anything behave differently than expected?”
- “Which task card feels most urgent now?”

---

## Wrap-Ups: Using the Project Guide as an Exit Ticket

You do not need separate exit tickets. Students update the **milestone sections** in their Project Guide each day.

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 20px 0px;">
  <p style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 12pt;">💡Teacher Tip</p>
  <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Look for <b>decision-making</b>, not just activity.</p>
  <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">If students write “we tested” or “we fixed bugs,” ask:</p>
  <ul>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">“What problem did this fix address?”</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">“What evidence told you this was the right change?”</li>
  </ul>
</div>

---

## Using Benchmarks as Formal Checkpoints

Benchmarks A, B, and C (Days 3, 5, and 8) are your most important structured check-ins.

<div style="background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 20px 0px;">
  
  <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Slow this moment down.</p>
  <ul>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">Pause coding</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">Review the Kanban board</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">Move task cards</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">Reflect on feedback</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">Rotate the Project Leader</li>
  </ul>
  <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">Only then should teams continue building.</p>
</div>




MARKDOWN
